BoxData[
StyleBox["\"\\n b) How do these quasi-isotherms compare with the experimental isotherms shown in Chapter 2 of McQuarrie\[CloseCurlyQuote]s Molecular Thermodynamics? (For example, compare to Figures 2.6 and 2.7; note that Tc of Ar is 150.95 K.) Discuss similarities and differences.\"",
RGBColor[0.07`,0.25`,0.6`],StripOnInput -> False],StandardForm],"Print",ExpressionUUID -> "4cf925f1-aabf-4507-ad38-21c74012690c"],Cell[
BoxData[
"\"At high temps such as 400K that are above Ar's critical temp. the curve is smooth and increases. This is also a key observation in the experimental isotherms. However, experimental isotherms look flat at the region that represents the gas to liquid phase transition. The simulation does not show this. \"",
StandardForm],"Print",ExpressionUUID -> "fa47266e-ad46-4822-b89d-36edf1442bba"],Cell[
BoxData[
StyleBox["\"\\n c) The article specifies that the MD simulations produce quasi-isotherms, not true isotherms. Based on the simulation results, why do you think that the article makes that point? (It might also help to look at the initial and final temperatures in the \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]FILENAME_output.txt\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] results file for a few simulations.)\"",
RGBColor[0.07`,0.25`,0.6`],StripOnInput -> False],StandardForm],"Print",ExpressionUUID -> "170bfb4b-b80c-4d5d-8a6b-49330813050e"],Cell[
BoxData[
"\"MD software simulates a constant Energy instead of constant Temperature. As the simulation runs, energy is both kinetic and potential. As PE changes (as particles get closer), measured temperature drifts. \"",
StandardForm],"Print",ExpressionUUID -> "af48c00e-4dcb-41eb-84c6-fa6fcf941604"],Cell[
BoxData[
StyleBox["\"\\n d)Consider your isotherm. How does pressure change with changes in number density? Give a physical explanation for this change. Pick a few number densities and inspect the animated trajectories for the simulations. Do you observe qualitative differences in molecular behavior that might explain the differences in pressure?\"",
RGBColor[0.07`,0.25`,0.6`],StripOnInput -> False],StandardForm],"Print",ExpressionUUID -> "1f7788c5-dd7c-4309-bbdf-528ae7d6d3d1"],Cell[
BoxData[
"\"As the number density increases, pressure increases exponentially. Pressure is the average force by the particles that undergo collisions divided by total area. As we increase the number density,there are more moles of gas in the same volume. This increases the frequency of the collisions therefore affecting pressure. As I inspect the trajectories (for ex. 35,000 mol/m^3), the system appears very cluttered. The particles are impacting the boundaries. This is the opposite of the 1 mol/m^3 run. \"",
StandardForm],"Print",ExpressionUUID -> "bd755a85-aa44-4222-a1c2-e760ae594779"],Cell[
BoxData[
StyleBox["\"\\n e) Pick a number density and compare the pressure for the simulations at the different temperatures. How does pressure change with temperature? Give a physical explanation for this change. Do you observe qualitative differences in molecular behavior that might explain the differences in pressure?\"",
RGBColor[0.07`,0.25`,0.6`],StripOnInput -> False],StandardForm],"Print",ExpressionUUID -> "bafb21e0-69ef-4595-b35d-9788d900d549"],Cell[
BoxData[
"\"Upon fixing number density, increasing temperature also increases the pressure. Considering that temperature is proportional to the mean squared velocity, then, at higher temps particles must be traveling faster. Consequently, they also carry momentum and strike harder against the walls. Imparting a larger force over the same area results in a higher pressure. At 400k, particles move rapidly. At 10k they move much slower.\"",
StandardForm],"Print",ExpressionUUID -> "1291014b-f861-43d1-abfb-b0d787d2a0ae"],Cell[
BoxData[
InterpretationBox[RowBox[{"\"\\n\"","\[InvisibleSpace]",StyleBox["\"f) Pick one isotherm and use the ThermodynamicData function in Mathematica to calculate the molar density for each of the pressure values in that isotherm. Compare the experimental isotherm from Mathematica to the one obtained in your simulation. Discuss any differences. \"",
RGBColor[0.07`,0.25`,0.6`],StripOnInput -> False]}],SequenceForm["\n",Style["f) Pick one isotherm and use the ThermodynamicData function in Mathematica to calculate the molar density for each of the pressure values in that isotherm. Compare the experimental isotherm from Mathematica to the one obtained in your simulation. Discuss any differences. ",
RGBColor[0.07`,0.25`,0.6`]]],Editable -> False],StandardForm],"Print",ExpressionUUID -> "85043bef-1122-452a-bdd1-e0639962a4b4"],Cell[
BoxData[
"\"\"",StandardForm],"Print",ExpressionUUID -> "aebb77f7-1567-4e60-8d3d-cfdcc2c19205"],Cell[
